6 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055536 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055537 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055536 #edb/ref 1061 13785 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055536 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1d9fa15b-505e-5efb-acca-ed49130fabb6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055536 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055537 #edb/ref 1061 13932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055537 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5aabf84e-eb5f-5695-bef0-ba3fb10691b5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055537 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054617 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055965 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's remind ourselves of the first few powers of $\\textrm{i}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{i}}^{1} & =\\text{i} \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{3} & =-\\text{i} \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{4} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the fourth power, we return to the value of $1,$ which can also be written as $\\textrm{i}^0 = 1.$ This means that if we continue to increase the powers, we would cycle through the same four values once again, as shown below.\n\n![](images/5e/5e3f3a42dd591daf109e474eec99e446706e0d8e46f1252540839cd50d957463.png)\n\nSo, for $\\textrm{i}^5$ through $\\textrm{i}^8,$ the exact same pattern repeats. Remembering that $\\textrm{i}^4 = 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\textrm{i}^5&=\\textrm{i}^4 \\cdot \\textrm{i} &= \\textrm{i}\\[5pt]\n\t\t\\textrm{i}^6&=\\textrm{i}^4 \\cdot \\textrm{i}^2 &=-1\\[5pt]\n\t\t\\textrm{i}^7&=\\textrm{i}^4 \\cdot \\textrm{i}^3 &= -\\textrm{i} \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\textrm{i}^8&=\\textrm{i}^4 \\cdot \\textrm{i}^4 &= 1 .\n\t}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, for $\\textrm{i}^9$ through to $\\textrm{i}^{12},$ the pattern repeats again:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\textrm{i}^9&=(\\textrm{i}^4)^2 \\cdot \\textrm{i} &= \\textrm{i}\\[5pt]\n\t\t\\textrm{i}^{10}&=(\\textrm{i}^4)^2 \\cdot \\textrm{i}^2 &=-1\\[5pt]\n\t\t\\textrm{i}^{11}&=(\\textrm{i}^4)^2 \\cdot \\textrm{i}^3 &= -\\textrm{i} \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\textrm{i}^{12}&=(\\textrm{i}^4)^2 \\cdot \\textrm{i}^4 &= 1 \n\t}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to simplify a higher integer power of $\\textrm{i},$ we *divide the power by $4$ and raise $\\textrm{i}$ to the remainder*.\n\nLet's see this idea in action." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055965 #edb/ref 1065 2762 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055965 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "be735724-c1e7-5d1c-b396-61d58ee81903" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055965 #edb/ref 1067 "The Cyclic Nature of Powers of i" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055966 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's compute the first few powers of $\\textrm{i},$ where $\\textrm{i} = \\sqrt{-1}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{i}}^{0} & =1 \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{1} & =\\text{i} \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{3} & ={\\text{i}}^{2}\\cdot \\text{i}=(-1)\\cdot \\text{i}=-\\text{i} \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{4} & ={\\text{i}}^{2}\\cdot {\\text{i}}^{2}=(-1)\\cdot (-1)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use these results to evaluate expressions containing powers of $\\textrm{i}.$ Let's take a look at some examples." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055966 #edb/ref 1065 2763 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055966 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "6fe2451a-f8be-5178-a84f-66817536929d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055966 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537206 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186538012 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537206 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186538016 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537206 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186538026 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537206 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186538040 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537206 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186538058 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537206 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537206 #edb/ref 1045 "The Cyclic Property of the Imaginary Unit" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537206 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186538040 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537206 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046647 #edb/ref 1